WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.174
In part one,

00:00:01.174 --> 00:00:03.652
the stack lived in a linked list.

00:00:03.652 --> 00:00:06.858
This time we keep the elements in an array

00:00:06.858 --> 00:00:09.700
with the bottom element at index zero.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:12.640
One integer field, topOfStack,

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.311
is the index of the top element.

00:00:15.311 --> 00:00:17.982
The array itself is a field too.

00:00:17.982 --> 00:00:20.815
An empty stack has nothing on top,

00:00:20.815 --> 00:00:23.810
so topOfStack starts at -1.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.960
The number of elements is therefore

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.360
always topOfStack + 1.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.440
And MIN_STACK_SIZE, 5,

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.440
is the smallest capacity

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.280
we will ever allocate.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:36.925
Careful here,

00:00:37.007 --> 00:00:40.284
the slides say that topOfStack records

00:00:40.366 --> 00:00:43.315
the number of elements in the stack.

00:00:43.315 --> 00:00:44.380
It does not.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:48.492
Put 1, 2 and 3 into

00:00:48.492 --> 00:00:49.746
cells 0, 1 and 2.

00:00:49.746 --> 00:00:52.834
The stack holds three elements,

00:00:52.834 --> 00:00:55.245
but topOfStack is 2,

00:00:55.245 --> 00:00:57.850
the index of the top cell.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.106
That is why push writes to array[++topOfStack]:

00:01:01.196 --> 00:01:04.919
move up first,

00:01:04.919 --> 00:01:06.009
then store.

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:08.460
And pop reads array[topOfStack--]:

00:01:08.551 --> 00:01:11.547
read first,

00:01:11.547 --> 00:01:13.000
then move down.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:15.332
The constructor takes the

00:01:15.402 --> 00:01:16.803
capacity we ask for,

00:01:16.873 --> 00:01:19.115
but never makes an array smaller

00:01:19.185 --> 00:01:20.587
than MIN_STACK_SIZE.

00:01:20.657 --> 00:01:22.618
Asking for a capacity of 2

00:01:22.688 --> 00:01:24.300
still gives 5 cells.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:26.961
Java cannot create an array

00:01:27.047 --> 00:01:28.759
of a type parameter,

00:01:28.845 --> 00:01:31.584
so we create an Object array and

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:33.810
cast it to an array of E.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:37.316
The stack is full when the top element

00:01:37.393 --> 00:01:39.087
sits in the last cell:

00:01:39.164 --> 00:01:41.089
topOfStack == array.length - 1,

00:01:41.166 --> 00:01:43.630
here 4.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:45.319
Here is push.

00:01:45.319 --> 00:01:48.731
If the stack is full, it returns false (overflow),

00:01:48.731 --> 00:01:51.546
and nothing is stored.

00:01:51.546 --> 00:01:55.128
Otherwise, it executes array[++topOfStack]

00:01:55.213 --> 00:01:57.090
= x.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.774
It returns true to say the

00:01:59.864 --> 00:02:01.569
element was stored.

00:02:01.569 --> 00:02:05.249
The ++ comes before topOfStack

00:02:05.249 --> 00:02:08.840
so the index moves up before the write.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.374
A classic slip is to write array[

00:02:12.459 --> 00:02:15.076
topOfStack++] instead.

00:02:15.076 --> 00:02:18.874
Postfix ++ stores x at the old index

00:02:18.874 --> 00:02:20.900
and only then moves up.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:23.126
Starting from -1

00:02:23.204 --> 00:02:25.236
the very first push writes

00:02:25.314 --> 00:02:27.033
to array[-1]

00:02:27.112 --> 00:02:29.456
and Java throws an ArrayIndex

00:02:29.534 --> 00:02:31.410
OutOfBoundsException.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.335
Prefix ++ in push

00:02:34.335 --> 00:02:37.219
postfix -- in pop

00:02:37.219 --> 00:02:40.000
the two are mirror images.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:43.962
Trace three pushes on the empty stack.

00:02:43.962 --> 00:02:44.852
Push 1

00:02:44.852 --> 00:02:48.678
topOfStack moves from -1 to 0

00:02:48.678 --> 00:02:51.526
and 1 is stored in cell 0.

00:02:51.526 --> 00:02:53.928
Push 2 goes to cell 1

00:02:53.928 --> 00:02:56.420
and push 3 to cell 2.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:58.885
Each push is a comparison,

00:02:58.885 --> 00:03:01.651
an increment and one array write,

00:03:01.651 --> 00:03:04.010
whatever the size: O(1).

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:07.192
top returns array[topOfStack]

00:03:07.192 --> 00:03:09.870
after the same empty check as pop.

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:13.084
Here it reads cell 2 and returns 3

00:03:13.084 --> 00:03:15.150
without changing anything.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:18.698
pop first remembers the top element

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:21.337
x = array[topOfStack].

00:03:21.419 --> 00:03:23.893
Then it sets that cell to null

00:03:23.975 --> 00:03:26.284
and moves topOfStack down

00:03:26.284 --> 00:03:27.850
and returns 3.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.959
The slide version is just return array

00:03:31.032 --> 00:03:33.065
[topOfStack--].

00:03:33.065 --> 00:03:36.187
It works, but it leaves a reference to the

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:38.220
popped object in the array

00:03:38.293 --> 00:03:41.124
so the garbage collector cannot reclaim

00:03:41.197 --> 00:03:43.593
it until the cell is overwritten.

00:03:43.593 --> 00:03:46.280
Setting the cell to null fixes that

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.240
and pop is still O(1).

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:51.466
The demo asks for an ArrayStack

00:03:51.552 --> 00:03:52.918
of capacity 2

00:03:52.918 --> 00:03:56.162
which the constructor raises to 5.

00:03:56.247 --> 00:03:58.808
Pushes 1 to 5 all succeed

00:03:58.894 --> 00:04:00.516
and fill the array.

00:04:00.516 --> 00:04:03.674
The sixth push finds the stack full

00:04:03.674 --> 00:04:07.003
it returns false, and nothing changes.

00:04:07.003 --> 00:04:08.540
That is overflow.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:10.524
top gives 5.

00:04:10.524 --> 00:04:14.734
pop gives 5, and the next pop gives 4.

00:04:14.734 --> 00:04:18.383
This toString prints the bottom first

00:04:18.383 --> 00:04:19.880
like the array.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:23.377
makeEmpty clears the cells one by one

00:04:23.377 --> 00:04:24.784
which is O(n).

00:04:24.784 --> 00:04:28.180
After that, a pop finds the stack empty

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:29.670
and returns null.

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:32.661
A fixed capacity is a nuisance

00:04:32.661 --> 00:04:34.760
too small and pushes fail

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:37.249
too large and memory is wasted.

00:04:37.249 --> 00:04:39.970
A dynamic array removes the limit.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:44.049
Here is a growing stack with capacity 4

00:04:44.049 --> 00:04:46.563
already holding 1 to 4.

00:04:46.563 --> 00:04:48.731
push(5) finds it full.

00:04:48.731 --> 00:04:51.159
So push first calls resize

00:04:51.159 --> 00:04:53.153
with twice the length,

00:04:53.153 --> 00:04:55.581
a new array of eight cells.

00:04:55.581 --> 00:04:58.616
The four elements are copied over

00:04:58.616 --> 00:04:59.657
one by one.

00:04:59.657 --> 00:05:03.559
The array field now refers to the new array

00:05:03.559 --> 00:05:06.160
and only then is 5 stored.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:09.310
resize itself is a short method.

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:12.120
A loop copies cells 0 to topOfStack

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.449
into the bigger array,

00:05:14.449 --> 00:05:17.580
and then the field array refers to it.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:22.073
The growing demo pushes 1 to 17

00:05:22.073 --> 00:05:24.840
starting from capacity 4.

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:28.462
How many element copies happen in total?

00:05:28.462 --> 00:05:30.670
Pause the video and count.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:37.696
push(5) copies 4 elements.

00:05:37.696 --> 00:05:40.364
push(9) copies 8.

00:05:40.364 --> 00:05:43.810
push(17) copies 16.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:47.095
So 17 pushes cause 28

00:05:47.173 --> 00:05:49.026
element copies in total.

00:05:49.026 --> 00:05:52.116
That is fewer than 2 * 17,

00:05:52.116 --> 00:05:53.120
34.

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:54.381
In general,

00:05:54.381 --> 00:05:57.941
after a copy to a capacity of 2k,

00:05:58.023 --> 00:06:00.010
the next copy comes only

00:06:00.093 --> 00:06:01.997
after another k pushes.

00:06:01.997 --> 00:06:03.487
So over n pushes,

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:06.799
the copies move at most 4 + 8

00:06:06.882 --> 00:06:09.117
and so on up to n elements,

00:06:09.117 --> 00:06:11.269
which is less than 2n.

00:06:11.269 --> 00:06:13.339
For 33 pushes,

00:06:13.422 --> 00:06:15.740
4 + 8 + 16

00:06:15.823 --> 00:06:17.892
+ 32 is 60,

00:06:17.892 --> 00:06:19.713
fewer than 66.

00:06:19.713 --> 00:06:21.866
One push can cost O(n),

00:06:21.866 --> 00:06:23.936
but the total is O(n),

00:06:23.936 --> 00:06:26.750
so on average a push is O(1).

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:30.310
We say push takes amortized O(1) time.

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:33.260
The array must grow by a factor

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:34.804
not by a constant.

00:06:34.886 --> 00:06:38.136
Growing by one cell at a time would copy

00:06:38.217 --> 00:06:40.736
1 + 2 and so on up to n

00:06:40.736 --> 00:06:43.580
about n²/2 elements.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.168
Popping goes the other way.

00:06:46.168 --> 00:06:47.729
To give memory back,

00:06:47.803 --> 00:06:50.182
a pop that leaves the array only

00:06:50.257 --> 00:06:52.116
a quarter full halves it.

00:06:52.116 --> 00:06:54.718
After the demo's 17 pushes,

00:06:54.792 --> 00:06:57.246
popping down to size 8 halves

00:06:57.320 --> 00:06:58.956
32 to 16.

00:06:58.956 --> 00:07:01.558
Size 4 halves 16 to 8,

00:07:01.558 --> 00:07:04.160
and size 2 halves 8 to 4.

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:06.974
Why a quarter, and not one half?

00:07:07.051 --> 00:07:09.130
Suppose the array halved as

00:07:09.207 --> 00:07:11.132
soon as it was half full.

00:07:11.132 --> 00:07:13.288
Take a full array of 8.

00:07:13.288 --> 00:07:15.444
A push grows it to 16,

00:07:15.521 --> 00:07:17.754
a pop leaves it half full and

00:07:17.831 --> 00:07:19.679
halves it back to 8,

00:07:19.679 --> 00:07:22.297
and the next push grows it again.

00:07:22.374 --> 00:07:24.222
Every push and every pop

00:07:24.299 --> 00:07:26.070
copies the whole array.

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:28.012
With the quarter rule,

00:07:28.084 --> 00:07:30.254
that pop leaves 8 elements

00:07:30.327 --> 00:07:33.220
in 16 cells, and nothing is copied.

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:35.789
So, linked list or array?

00:07:35.868 --> 00:07:38.494
The slides say a list saves space

00:07:38.573 --> 00:07:40.403
and an array is faster.

00:07:40.403 --> 00:07:43.346
Each linked node stores the element,

00:07:43.346 --> 00:07:46.449
a next reference, and an object header,

00:07:46.449 --> 00:07:49.949
and a push allocates a new node every time.

00:07:49.949 --> 00:07:53.051
An array cell stores only the element,

00:07:53.051 --> 00:07:56.154
and the array is one contiguous block.

00:07:56.154 --> 00:07:58.938
When the processor loads one cell,

00:07:58.938 --> 00:08:02.120
its neighbors come into the cache too.

00:08:02.470 --> 00:08:03.649
On the other hand,

00:08:03.649 --> 00:08:06.595
a list never reserves cells it does not use,

00:08:06.661 --> 00:08:08.822
while a growing array can have up

00:08:08.887 --> 00:08:11.244
to three quarters of its cells empty

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:12.750
right after shrinking.

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:15.586
Both are O(1) per operation,

00:08:15.586 --> 00:08:18.693
so the difference is a constant factor,

00:08:18.693 --> 00:08:19.936
but a real one.

00:08:19.936 --> 00:08:23.431
In practice, array-based stacks with growing

00:08:23.509 --> 00:08:25.140
are the usual choice.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:27.719
In real code you will rarely

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:29.289
write your own stack.

00:08:29.289 --> 00:08:32.215
java.util.Stack is an old class

00:08:32.287 --> 00:08:34.571
and its documentation recommends

00:08:34.642 --> 00:08:37.783
the Deque interface instead, with ArrayDeque

00:08:37.783 --> 00:08:39.638
a growing circular array.

00:08:39.710 --> 00:08:41.423
Its push and pop work on

00:08:41.494 --> 00:08:43.064
the front of the Deque

00:08:43.064 --> 00:08:44.920
so it behaves as a stack.

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:49.583
After pushing A, B and C, it prints C, B, A

00:08:49.583 --> 00:08:51.347
and peek gives C.

00:08:51.347 --> 00:08:54.288
Pop gives C, leaving B and A.

00:08:54.288 --> 00:08:58.110
And peek on an empty Deque gives null.

00:08:58.460 --> 00:08:59.880
Unlike our classes

00:08:59.955 --> 00:09:03.093
the library's pop on an empty Deque throws

00:09:03.168 --> 00:09:05.260
a NoSuchElementException.

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:06.774
To sum up.

00:09:06.774 --> 00:09:09.667
One: topOfStack is the index

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:11.534
of the top element

00:09:11.534 --> 00:09:14.800
minus one when the stack is empty

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:18.440
and the size is topOfStack plus one.

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:22.360
Two: push moves up and stores; pop reads

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:25.347
clears the cell and moves down.

00:09:25.347 --> 00:09:27.120
Both are O(1).

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:30.657
Three: a fixed array can overflow.

00:09:30.657 --> 00:09:33.376
A doubling array never does

00:09:33.376 --> 00:09:36.750
and its push is amortised O(1).

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:37.999
Try this.

00:09:37.999 --> 00:09:42.195
Create an ArrayStack with capacity 3

00:09:42.195 --> 00:09:45.592
then push(7), push(8), pop

00:09:45.592 --> 00:09:48.389
and push(9), push(10), push(11)

00:09:48.389 --> 00:09:50.487
push(12) and push(13).

00:09:50.487 --> 00:09:53.084
Which push returns false

00:09:53.084 --> 00:09:57.180
and what is topOfStack at that moment?

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:58.748
In part three

00:09:58.748 --> 00:10:02.490
a stack checks that brackets are balanced.

